<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Prefect</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as prefects</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A prefect is an official placed in charge of an administrative unit or service. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> prefects held major military and administrative posts, including the governorship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-colonial-empire">French Empire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a> was organised into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a> administered by prefects after 1848, like metropolitan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/prefect.jpg" alt="Prefect" width="320" height="405" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gzen92 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pr%C3%A9fet_Louis_Laugier_(Colmar).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
